\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Graph connections are classified by holonomy}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Let #{\Gamma} be a finite connected graph with chosen edge
orientations and root #{o}. For a path traversing #{e_1,\ldots,e_k},
use inverse edge values when traversing backwards and define its
\newvocab{holonomy} by
##{U_{e_k}\cdots U_{e_1}.}
Thus the rightmost factor transports first. Use the same composition
convention for loops: #{\beta\alpha} first traverses #{\alpha} and
then #{\beta}. The gauge classes of #{G}-valued connections are
##{\operatorname{Hom}(\pi_1(\Gamma,o),G)/G
\;\cong\;G^{b_1}/G,\qquad b_1=|E|-|V|+1,}
where #{G} acts by simultaneous conjugation. The second identification
depends on a spanning tree and orientations of the remaining edges.
This classifies group-valued connections, not their possibly nonfaithful
representation images or the spectra of their precisions.}

\proof{
\p{Choose a spanning tree #{T}. Each vertex #{v} has a unique tree
path from #{o}; let #{t_v\in G} be its transport, with #{t_o=1}.
Use the gauge #{g_v=t_v^{-1}}. If a tree edge runs from #{s} to #{h},
its path transports satisfy #{t_h=U_et_s}, with the equivalent inverse
identity when the rooted path traverses the edge backwards. Hence
##{(gU)_e=t_h^{-1}U_et_s=1\qquad(e\in T).}
Every connection therefore has a representative with all tree values
one. The remaining #{|E|-|T|=b_1} edge values are unrestricted.}

\p{A gauge transformation carrying one such representative to another
satisfies #{g_hg_s^{-1}=1} on every tree edge. Tree connectivity forces
all #{g_v} to equal one element #{a\in G}. It conjugates every
remaining edge value by #{a}. Conversely every simultaneous conjugation
comes from this constant gauge. This proves the classification by
#{G^{b_1}/G}, including the case of a tree, where #{b_1=0}.}

\p{Traversing a path and then its inverse gives cancelling group
factors, so transport is unchanged by removing backtracking. The loops
that run from #{o} through the tree to the source of a non-tree edge,
traverse it and return through the tree are a free basis of
#{\pi_1(\Gamma,o)}. This is
\citet{Proposition 1A.2 and its proof, pp. 84–85}{hatcher2002algebraic}:
contract the tree to obtain a wedge of #{b_1} circles. Assigning arbitrary
values to this free basis gives every group homomorphism. In tree gauge
these values are precisely the non-tree edge values. A general gauge
conjugates each rooted loop holonomy by #{g_o}, proving the first
identification as well.}
}

\p{With [two-cells attached](fdip-004N), call a connection
\newvocab{flat} when the ordered holonomy around every attaching
boundary is the identity in #{G}. Equivalently the graph homomorphism
kills the normal subgroup generated by those boundaries. It therefore
factors through the fundamental group of the resulting space. A representation
#{\rho} can send a nonidentity group element to the identity operator;
operator flatness alone is therefore insufficient when #{\rho} is
nonfaithful.}

\p{In the representation on #{F}, a parallel section is determined by
its root value #{x\in F}. Tree transport defines its other values, and
each non-tree edge condition says that its rooted holonomy fixes #{x}.
Consequently evaluation at #{o} identifies the space of parallel
sections with the common fixed space of the holonomy representation.
This proves the massless-kernel criterion of \ref{fdip-006D} in terms
of cycle data.}
